# Reducing energy loss via tuning energy levels of polymer acceptors for efficient all-polymer solar cells

Huiliang Sun1,8†, Bin Liu1,6†, Jianwei $\mathrm{Yu}^{2}$ , Xianshao ${Z_{0}}\mathrm{u}^{4}$ , Guangye Zhang3, Yujie Zhang1, Wei Zhang7, Mengyao $\mathrm{Su}^{1}$ , Qunping Fan5, Kun Yang1, Jianhua Chen1, He Yan8, Feng Gao2 & Xugang Guo
1Department of Materials Science and Engineering, Southern University of Science and Technology, Shenzhen 518055, China; 2Department of Physics, Chemistry and Biology (IFM), Linköping University, Linköping SE-58183, Sweden; 3eFlexPV Limited, Flat/RM B, 12/F, Hang Seng Causeway Bay BLDG, Causeway Bay, Hong Kong, China; 4Division of Chemical Physics, Lund University Box 124, 221 00 Lund, Sweden; 5Department of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Chalmers University of Technology, Göteborg SE-412 96, Sweden;   
6School of Advanced Materials, Peking University Shenzhen Graduate School, Peking University, Shenzhen 518055, China; 7School of Physics and Electronic Engineering, Guangzhou University, Guangzhou 510006, China;   
8Department of Chemistry and Hong Kong Branch of Chinese National Engineering Research Center for Tissue Restoration & Reconstruction, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Clear Water Bay, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China
The open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of all-polymer solar cells (all-PSCs) is typically lower than $0.9\mathrm{V}$ even for the most efficient ones. Large energy loss is the main reason for limiting $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and efficiency of all-PSCs. Herein, through materials design using electron deficient building blocks based on bithiophene imides, the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy levels of polymer acceptors can be effectively tuned, which resulted in a reduced energy loss induced by charge generation and recombination loss due to the suppressed charge-transfer (CT) state absorption. Despite a negligible driving force, all-PSC based on the polymer donor and acceptor combination with well-aligned energy levels exhibited efficient charge transfer and achieved an external quantum efficiency over $70\%$ while maintaining a large $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.02\mathrm{V},$ leading to a $9.21\%$ efficiency. Through various spectroscopy approaches, this work sheds light on the mechanism of energy loss in all-PSCs, which paves an avenue to achieving efficient all-PSCs with large $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and drives the further development of all-PSCs.
## all-polymer solar cells, polymer acceptors, energy level modulation, photovoltage, energy loss
Citation: Sun H, Liu B, Yu J, Zou X, Zhang G, Zhang Y, Zhang W, Su M, Fan Q, Yang K, Chen J, Yan H, Gao F, Guo X. Reducing energy loss via tuning energy levels of polymer acceptors for efficient all-polymer solar cells. Sc China Chem, 2020, 63, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-020-9826-4
## 1 Introduction
All-polymer solar cells (all-PSCs), consisting of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\tt~p}}$ -conjugated polymers as both electron donor and acceptor in the active layer, have attracted considerable attention due to the tunable energetics and chemical structures of both donor and acceptor materials. These properties, along with the long backbone for charge transport (compared to small molecules), provide all-PSCs with multiple favorable features including strong absorption across a wide spectral range, large charge carrier mobilities, high morphological stability, and superior mechanical durability [1–7]. Very recently, the power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of all-PSCs have been increased to over $13\%$ mainly benefiting from the incorporation of fused ring electron acceptor into polymer backbones [8–10]. However, the limiting parameter for achieving higher PCEs is the low open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ , which is seldom exceed $1.0\mathrm{V}$ in efficient all-PSCs (Figure 1 (a) and Table S1, Supporting Information online). For instance, the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of several high-performance all-PSCs based on fully conjugated polymer acceptors that showed PCEs over $8\%$ are only around $0.85\mathrm{V}$ [11–14]. For those all-PSCs that demonstrated high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ (around $1.0\mathrm{V},$ , the PCEs rarely surpass $8\%$ (Figure 1(a) and Table S1) [15–17]. Reducing the energy offsets and achieving high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ (Figure 1(b)) usually lead to a decrease short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ , especially for all-PSCs (Figure 1(c)) [18,19]. Two impressive examples have been reported by Wang group [15] and Kim group [20], where high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 1.06 and $1.1\mathrm{V}$ along with PCEs of $6.6\%$ and $8.0\%$ were realized by polymer donors that were tailored to have lower-lying highest occupied molecular orbitals (HOMOs), respectively, while $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ are limited to ${\sim}11\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure 1(c) and Table S1) due to insufficient charge separation. On the other hand, the highperformance polymer acceptors, which are mainly based on arene diimide such as naphthalene diimide (NDI) [21], showed low $V_{\mathrm{oc}}(<0.88\:\mathrm{V})$ even in the most efficient all-PSC devices [22,23]. It is found that the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy levels $(E_{\mathrm{LUMO}})$ of these NDI polymers are determined by the NDI unit due to the localized LUMO on this unit and hence cannot be easily tuned [5].
There have been very few attempts to develop polymer acceptors with tunable energy levels to improve the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of all-PCSs mainly due to limited electron-deficient building blocks. The most representative ones, organoboron polymer acceptors, developed by Liu and co-workers [16] showed high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ enabled by tunable energy levels (blue dots in Figure 1(a)) [5]. The only example is that a large $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.17\mathrm{V}$ and a high PCE of $10.1\%$ have been achieved by a new organoboron polymer, very recently. However, all-PSCs based on organoboron polymers still suffer from relatively low external quantum efficiency (EQE, $<65\%$ ) and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ even for the best-performing one [16], and processing additives are usually required for realizing an optimized morphology, which is not favorable for the large-area and low-cost fabrication of organic solar cells (OSCs) [24]. Additionally, it is very difficult to synthesize and purify organoboron polymers due to the suboptimal stability of boron-nitrogen coordination bond. Hence, it is highly needed to design and synthesize polymer acceptors with tunable frontier molecular orbital (FMO) to overcome the trade-off between $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ [25].
One of the most important factors that limits $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and PCEs of all-PSCs is energy loss $(E_{\mathrm{loss}})$ , which is defined as $E_{\mathrm{loss}}{=}E_{\mathrm{g}}$ $-q V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ where $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ is the bandgap of the absorber (Figure 1(b)) [26–29]. Compared to other types of OSCs, the understanding of $E_{\mathrm{loss}}$ and charge transfer mechanism of all-PSCs lags behind due to the lack of suitable polymer/polymer combinations [2,30]. Recent studies demonstrated that reducing $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ via minimizing energetic offset between donor and acceptor even to zero for efficient polymer/small-molecule solar cells have been achieved [31,32], but the detailed loss mechanism remain unclear despite that small $E_{\mathrm{loss}}$ are observed in a few polymer/polymer combinations [2,12,16,24,33].
Herein, we develop two new polymer acceptors, enabling efficient all-PSC devices with a large $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ while maintaining high $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , and systematically investigate the $E_{\mathrm{loss}}$ and electron transfer processes in all-PSCs in detail. Employing the recently developed electron-deficient building blocks (BTIn) [25,34], two new BTIn polymer acceptors SPA1 and SPA2 were designed and synthesized (Figure 2(a) and Scheme S1, Supporting Information online) by integrating successful molecular design strategies of BTIn polymers [35–38]. The BTIn polymers are investigated in terms of the synthesis, structure-property correlation and device performance by Osaka and our group [25], independently. These polymers have different electronic structures from the typical NDI polymer acceptors, featuring tunable $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ due to their delocalized LUMO profiles over the polymer backbones. As a result, the electronic structure of our polymer acceptor can be tuned to be compatible with the polymer donor. Particularly, we can tune the $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ to make the $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ offset between the new polymer acceptor and the polymer donor negligible to maximize the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ , which also afforded an excellent opportunity to investigate the origins of energy loss in all-PSCs. Through Fourier-transform photocurrent spectroscopy EQE (FTPS-EQE) and electroluminescence (EL) experiments, we show that the PTB7-Th:SPA1 blend (Blend A) has distinct upshifted charge-transfer (CT) state compared to that of the benchmark NDI polymer based Blend C, resulting in allPSCs with a remarkable $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.15\mathrm{V}.$ However, the EQE was greatly limited due to a low degree of electron transfer and considerable charge recombination enabled by negligible $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ offset. Benefiting from the tunability of energy levels of the BTIn polymers, a large $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.02\mathrm{V}$ and a high EQE over $70\%$ were simultaneously achieved in all-PSCs based on PTB7-Th:SPA2 blend (Blend B) with a small $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ offset, leading to a remarkable PCE of $9.21\%$ , which is among the highest values reported for all-PSCs. Timeresolved photoluminescence measurements show that ultrafast charge transfer processes in BTIn-based all-PSCs can occur despite the extremely small driving force. In addition, both devices based on BTIn polymers exhibit relatively high external quantum efficiency of electroluminescence $\left(0.8\times10^{-3}\right.$ and ${0.1}\times{10}^{-4})$ ), which are much higher than that of the NDI polymer-based all-PSCs $(0.4{\times}10^{-\bar{7}})$ , suggesting a significantly low non-radiative recombination loss of ${<}0.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ which is comparable to that of the perovskite solar cells and has rarely been achieved in all-PSCs. These results demonstrate that energy level modulation of polymer acceptors is vital to reduce energy loss in the charge generation and non-radiative recombination processes, which paves an avenue to achieving efficient all-PSCs with large $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and drives the long-term development of all-PSCs.
