The morphology of Blend A and Blend B is characterized by two-dimensional grazing incident wide-angle $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray scattering (2D GIWAXS) and atom force microscopy (AFM). The 2D GIWAXS patterns and corresponding linecut profiles are shown in Figure S6. Both two blends exhibit a preferential face-on orientation, which is beneficial for charge transport in the vertical direction [48,49]. Blend B shows a larger $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking crystal coherence length (CCL, calculated from the full-width-at-half-maximum based on the Scherrer equation) $(1.06\mathrm{nm})$ than Blend A $(0.83\mathrm{nm})$ (Table S8), which agrees with the higher carrier mobility of Blend B. In addition, a smoother film surface is also observed in Blend B with a relatively small roughness of $0.73{\mathrm{nm}}$ compared to Blend A $(0.9\mathrm{nm})$ . The more favorable morphology in Blend B film not only contributes to the higher mobility but also facilitates the carrier extraction at the active layer-electrode interface (vide infra) [50].
## 2.3 Energy loss analysis
To further understand the origins of the high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and investigate the energy loss of all-PSCs, we analyzed the $E_{\mathrm{loss}}$ in detail for Blend A, Blend B and Blend C using various spectroscopy techniques such as FTPS-EQE and EL (Figure 4, Figure S7, and Table 2). Generally, the $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ in OSCs consists of the radiative recombination loss and the nonradiative recombination loss according to the detailed balance theory [30,51,52]. The first part $\Delta E_{\mathrm{1}}(E_{\mathrm{g}}-q V_{\mathrm{oc}}^{\mathrm{SQ}})$ is the radiative recombination loss, where $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ is the optical gap, $q$ is the elementary charge, and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}^{\mathrm{SQ}}$ is the maximum voltage by the Shockley-Queisser limit. The $\Delta E_{1}$ is unavoidable due to the absorption above the bandgap, which is ${\sim}0.28\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the three blends in this work. The second part $\Delta E_{2}$ $(q V_{\mathrm{oc}}^{\mathrm{SQ}}-q V_{\mathrm{oc}}^{\mathrm{rad}})$ in the radiative recombination loss, is negligible in inorganic and perovskite solar cells. In contrast, such loss in the conventional fullerene based OSCs is relatively high [52]. This situation has been changed with the emergence of NF-SMA based OSCs, recently [28,53–55], but in all-PSCs, the $\Delta E_{2}$ is estimated to be $0.17\mathrm{eV}$ for Blend C with the benchmark N2200 due to the distinctive sub-gap charge transfer (CT) state absorption. On the contrary, there is no distinctive CT absorption in Blend A and Blend B (Figure 4 (b) and Figure S7). As a result, $\Delta E_{2}$ for Blend A and Blend B are only 0.05 and $0.07\mathrm{eV}$ (Table 2), respectively, which is significantly smaller than that of Blend C $(0.17\mathrm{eV})$ and is also comparable to that of the state-of-the-art NF-SMA based OSCs and inorganic solar cells [56–58]. Notably, small $E_{\mathrm{g}}–E_{\mathrm{CT}}$ will be conducive to the hybridization of the CT state with the local exciton (LE) [56,59], leading to the increased radiation ability of the CT state and thus suppressing the nonradiative recombination $\Delta E_{3}$ $(q\Delta V_{\mathrm{oc}}^{\mathrm{non-rad}})$ from the CT state. The $E_{\mathrm{g}}–E_{\mathrm{CT}}$ is also considered to be the driving force for charge dissociation [52]. It was found that there is a good anti-correlation between the external quantum efficiency of electroluminescence $(\mathrm{EQE_{EL}})$ and the driving force value, i.e., the smaller driving force value results in an increased $\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}$ [56,60]. Accordingly, three blend films with gradually decreased driving forces show an increased $\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}$ at a different degree (Figure 4(c) and Figure S7). In addition, although almost a same driving force was observed in Blend A and Blend B, the corresponding SPA1 and SPA2 neat films possess distinct luminescence properties, which may lead to difference in the non-radiative losses (Figure S7(d, e)) [56]. In addition, time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) measurements demonstrate that Blend A has significantly lower quenching efficiency $(\eta)$ than Blend B (see below), which indicates that Blend A hold higher $\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}$ . Hence, using the equation of $\Delta E_{3}{=}{-}k T\mathrm{ln(EQE_{EL})}$ , the $\Delta E_{3}$ of Blend A, Blend B and Blend C were determined to be 0.16, 0.29 and $0.32\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 4(d)), respectively. The BTIn polymer-based devices yielded much smaller $\Delta E_{3}$ than the NDI polymer-based cells. Such small $\Delta E_{3}$ observed in blend A is comparable to that of perovskite solar cells. The $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ of Blend A and Blend B are accurately determined to be 1.65 and $1.66\mathrm{eV}$ by extracting from the derivatives of the EQE curve, respectively [61]. Overall, the values of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ calculated based on the radiative recombination and non-radiative recombination loss are consistent with the experimental results (Table 2).
## 2.4 Charge separation and transport
To gain insights into the electron transfer process, we first fabricated bilayer electron-only devices with a structure of ITO/ZnO/PTB7-Th/polymer acceptors $/\mathrm{Ca}/\mathrm{Al}$ [32,62]. For Blend A with a zero $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ offset, the corresponding $J_{-}V$ curves are symmetric, implying that electron transfer may occur in both directions (Figure 5(a, b)), which is not conducive to working all-PSC. In contrast, for Blend B with an $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ offset of $0.21\ \mathrm{eV},$ the current density values at positive bias are much larger than those at negative bias, which indicating that the electron transfer from PTB7-Th to SPA2 is allowed, but the opposite direction is forbidden (Figure 5(b)) [63], which is favorable for all-PSC operation.
The space-charge-limited current (SCLC) measurement (Table S9 and Figure $\mathbf{S}8(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}))$ also demonstrates that Blend B has higher electron mobility of $2.77{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and more balance hole/electron mobility ratio of 1.87 than those of Blend A $(1.57{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ ; 2.28). To more quantitively assess the effect of $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ offsets on the photoinduced charge transfer, we conducted TRPL measurements to gain further insights into the photoinduced exciton diffusion and dissociation dynamics in the BTIn polymer-based blends (see the supporting information (Supporting Information online) for more experimental details). The normalized spectra are shown in Figure $5(\mathrm{c},\mathrm{d})$ and Figure S9, and the average lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}})$ and $\eta$ are summarized in Table S10 and Figure S11. The BTIn polymer neat films show significantly longer PL $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ (ca.1000 ps) compared to that of N2200 (59 ps), which is more beneficial for exciton diffusion in the acceptor phase (Figure 5(c)) [44]. The $\eta$ of the polymer acceptors in both Blend A and Blend B can achieve very high value $(>94\%)$ due to large $E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}$ offset, which suggests that the exciton generated in the acceptor can effectively dissociate at the donor/acceptor interface (Figure S9 and Table S11). On the other hand, the $\eta$ of PTB7-Th in Blend A is only ca. $25\%$ , much lower than that in Blend B (ca. $74\%$ ) (Figure 5(d) and Table S11). The results demonstrate that the photoinduced electron transfer is suppressed to a greater extent and a relatively low degree of exciton dissociation occurs at SPA1 and PTB7-Th interface due to two-way electron transfer tendency enabled by zero $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ offset in Blend A. This is one of the reasons why Blend A-based all-PSCs show lower $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ than Blend B-based all-PSCs, thus resulting in an inferior PCE. we then measure the dependence of $\cdot_{J_{\mathrm{sc}}}$ on light intensity $(P_{\mathrm{light}})$ $(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\propto P_{\mathrm{light}}^{\mathrm{}})$ to evaluate bimolecular charge recombination (Figure S10(a)). As compared to Blend A based devices $\scriptstyle(a=0.971)$ , Blend B based devices show a higher $\scriptstyle a$ of 0.982, suggesting a lower degree of bimolecular recombination [64]. The photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ is plotted as a function of the effective voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ for two optimized devices to estimate the exciton dissociation in all-PSCs (Figure S10(b)). By using a high reverse bias, the saturated photocurrent $(J_{\mathrm{ph}},\mathsf{\Omega}_{\mathrm{sat}})$ can be determined [65]. The photocurrent at short circuit $(J_{\mathrm{ph}},\l_{\mathrm{sc}})$ for Blend A and Blend B based devices are 8.86 and $1\dot{5}.12\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively. Hence, compared to Blend B based device with a $J_{\mathrm{ph}},_{\mathrm{sc}}/J_{\mathrm{ph}},_{\mathrm{sat}}$ ratio of $97.02\%$ , this ratio for Blend A based device is only $92.24\%$ (Table S12), suggesting a better charge dissociation in Blend B based device. Moreover, the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}{-}P_{\mathrm{light}}$ plot (Figure S10(c)) shows a weak dependence of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ on $P_{\mathrm{light}}$ for Blend B based device with a low slope of $1.39\:k T/q$ , compared to Blend A based device $(1.50k T/q)$ . These results indicate that both the trap-assisted recombination and the second-order bimolecular recombination are weaker in Blend B based all-PSCs, which explains its high FF [66,67].
## 3 Conclusions
To conclude, we designed and synthesized two new polymer acceptors SPA1 and SPA2 with tunable LUMO levels based on the recently reported electron-deficient building blocks BTIn combined with the molecular design strategy, which greatly differ from the classical NDI based polymers with fixed LUMO levels. When applied in all-PSCs, The PTB7- Th:SPA1 (Blend A) film with zero $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ offset demonstrated a high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.15\mathrm{V}$ and a PCE of $4.46\%$ , but a moderate EQE response $(\sim39\%$ peak value) due to the supressed electron transfer and thus charge recombination. While Blend B with a small LUMO level offset showed ultrafast and efficient charge transfer despite a negligible driving force, yielding a high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.02\mathrm{V}$ and EQE over
$70\%$ simultaneously, which results in a PCE of $9.21\%$ without the aid of any processing additives. Such a result has rarely been achieved in any all-PSC systems and affords an excellent platform to investigate the origins of energy loss in all-PSCs. Various spectroscopic characterizations reveal that appropriately-positioned $E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ of polymer acceptor can not only effectively suppress radiative and non-radiative recombination loss but also significantly reduce driving force of charge separation. This study demonstrates that high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and effective charge generation can also be realized simultaneously in all-PSCs via fine-tuning the energy level of polymer acceptor. Our results show the critical role of developing polymer acceptors with tunable energy levels and offer important fundamental guidelines for developing more efficient all-PSCs.
